<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xenophanes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 570–c. 478 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Xenophanes of Colophon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xenophanes of Colophon was a Greek poet and philosopher who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> after the Persian conquest and spent a long life wandering the Greek world, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In surviving fragments of his verse he mocked the human-like gods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> and argued for a single, unmoving deity. He also reflected on the limits of human knowledge and on fossils as evidence of past seas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophanes.jpg" alt="Xenophanes" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xenophanes_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
